  "result": {
    "summary": "The fingerprint ignores event seq and timestamps, is stored on the record and launches entry in both _record_running and run_group, and suppresses only exited_early, gave_up and exited_ok records. failed_launch, infra_failure and skipped records never suppress. state_version, goal_launches, scouts_done and closable are untouched. The gate was green. Two gaps remain: the new test covers only part of the acceptance wording, and decision_points() now has side effects. I read the diff and ran nothing.",
    "verdict": "approve",
    "comments": [
      {
        "path": "tests/test_planner_runs.py",
        "line": 1266,
        "issue": "The acceptance criterion says the task is yielded again when hold_reason or resume_hint changes, or a fix-round child appears. The new test only exercises the hold_reason change, so the resume_hint and fix-round or respec or depends_on branches of hold_fingerprint are untested.",
        "severity": "low"
      },
      {
        "path": "orchestrator/planner_runs.py",
        "line": 227,
        "issue": "decision_points() now writes records (hold_unchanged_notified, _save_records) and sends a notification as a side effect, and hold_fingerprint calls bus.read() once per held candidate. A read-only caller such as a status or dry-run view would trigger notify and persist. The records list is also saved from a snapshot taken earlier in the generator, which could overwrite concurrent record updates. Acceptable for now, but fragile.",
        "severity": "low"
      }
    ],
